 Cursor runs planning with `--mode plan` and review and acceptance with `--mode ask`. Both are read-only, but in print mode `plan` produced no text for a review, which showed up as repeated `AUDIT_NO_VERDICT` results.
 
+**Read-only roles go only to CLIs that enforce them.** Planning, review, and acceptance must not change the repository. Codex runs them in its read-only sandbox, Claude gets only Read, Grep, and Glob, Cursor uses `plan`/`ask`, and Devin's `auto` mode auto-approves read-only tools only. AGY's `--mode plan` makes no such guarantee: as a reviewer it ran the project's CLI, used the network, and rewrote tracked files, so the session ended `BLOCKED` and lost a verified implementation (DOG-037). AUTO routing therefore skips AGY for those roles (`ROUTE AGY skipped: its CLI does not enforce read-only operation`); it still implements and remediates. An explicitly chosen AGY orchestrator still plans and accepts (explicit override) but never reviews. HowlPlane still checks every read-only attempt and blocks the session if one changed the repository.
+
+**Network for workers is opt-in.** `--worker-network` (on a new session or `resume`) requests network access for implementation and remediation. For Codex, it adds `-c sandbox_workspace_write.network_access=true` to the `workspace-write` sandbox; without the flag, HowlPlane leaves Codex's default sandbox network policy in place (DOG-038). Read-only roles never receive the setting. The other CLIs apply their own network policies; HowlPlane does not change their network configuration.
+
 A rejection judges one repository state. When the repository changes before `resume` (for example you fixed what the verdict named), review and acceptance exclusions that came from verdicts expire, the stale `audit` result is cleared, and the session is judged again after verification (pass `--verify`). If nothing changed, `resume` stops again with the same guidance. `orchestrate discard` starts over.
 
 Only the session orchestrator accepts, with one exception: if the orchestrator is disqualified for a reason unrelated to the work (capability, availability, capacity, as when its own implementation attempt was denied permission), the next eligible agent takes over, with a `TAKEOVER` event, so the session cannot deadlock. A verdict from any agent is final until the repository changes: a rejection is never shopped to another agent (see DOG-007 recovery above).
